class Solution {
    public void reverseQueue(Queue<Integer> q) {
        if(q.isEmpty()){
            return;                              // Base case: empty queue
        }
        
        int x = q.remove();                      // Remove front element
        reverseQueue(q);                         // Recursively reverse remaining
        q.add(x);                                // Add removed element to rear
    }
}

//////////////////////// DRY RUN /////////////////////////////
Initial: q=[5,10,15,20,25]

Call 1: x=5, q=[10,15,20,25] → Call 2
Call 2: x=10, q=[15,20,25] → Call 3
Call 3: x=15, q=[20,25] → Call 4
Call 4: x=20, q=[25] → Call 5
Call 5: x=25, q=[] → Call 6 (base case: return)

Unwinding:
Call 5: add 25 → q=[25]
Call 4: add 20 → q=[25,20]
Call 3: add 15 → q=[25,20,15]
Call 2: add 10 → q=[25,20,15,10]
Call 1: add 5 → q=[25,20,15,10,5]

Final: q=[25,20,15,10,5]

//////////////////////// QUESTION /////////////////////////////
Queue Reversal

Given a queue q containing integer elements, your task is to reverse the queue.

Examples:
Input: q[] = [5, 10, 15, 20, 25]
Output: [25, 20, 15, 10, 5]
Explanation: After reversing the queue, elements are in opposite order (front becomes rear).

Input: q[] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
Output: [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]
Explanation: After reversal, front becomes 5 and rear becomes 1.

Constraints:
1 ≤ q.size() ≤ 10^3
0 ≤ q[i] ≤ 10^5
